    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d525">

                <pb facs="0550" n="538" sameAs="frus:frus1945v08/pg538"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">891.51A/1–845: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Iran</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Morris</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Tehran</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-01-08T17:00:00+03:25">January 8, 1945—5
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received January 9—5:30 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>18. Medjliss<anchor n="98" xml:id="d525-f1"/> today passed bill<anchor n="99"
                        xml:id="d525-f2"/> canceling as of this date the special economic powers
                    granted Millspaugh<anchor n="1" xml:id="d525-f3"/> by the law of 13
                        Ordibehesht.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d525-f4"/>
                    <pb facs="0551" n="539" xml:id="pg539"/> Council of Ministers is authorized to
                    dissolve or reallocate the economic organizations which were under Millspaugh
                    administration by virtue of that law and to annul change or retain all
                    regulations issued thereunder.</p>
                <p>Cabinet is directed to create high consultative committee for economic affairs to
                    prepare within 3 months an economic plan in conformity with the needs of the
                    country.</p>
                <p>Vote on bill was 69 to 6.</p>
                <p>Repeated to Cairo for AEMME<anchor n="3" xml:id="d525-f5"/> and Hoskins.<anchor
                        n="4" xml:id="d525-f6"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Morris</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d525-f1" n="98" xml:id="d525-f1-content">
                <p>The Iranian Parliament.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d525-f2" n="99" xml:id="d525-f2-content">
                <p>Text transmitted to the Department in despatch 177, January 12, 1945, from
                    Tehran, not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d525-f3" n="1" xml:id="d525-f3-content">
                <p>Dr. Arthur O. Millspaugh, American Administrator General of the Finances in the
                    Iranian Government.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d525-f4" n="2" xml:id="d525-f4-content">
                <p>For substance of the law of 13 Ordibehesht, 1322 (May 4, 1943), see telegram 386,
                    April 14, 1943, 5 p.m., from Tehran, <ref target="frus1943v04#pg_522">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, 1943, vol. <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">iv</hi>, p. 522</ref>. The text was transmitted
                    to the Department in despatch 556, May 20, 1943, from Tehran (not printed), and
                    has been published by Dr. Millspaugh in his <hi rendition="#italic">Americans in
                        Persia</hi> (Brookings Institution, 1946), p. 273.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d525-f5" n="3" xml:id="d525-f5-content">
                <p>American Economic Mission in the Middle East.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d525-f6" n="4" xml:id="d525-f6-content">
                <p>Lt. Col. Harold B. Hoskins, Adviser on Economic Affairs, with the rank of
                    Counselor, at Legation in Egypt; assigned concurrently to Missions in Ethiopia,
                    Iran, Iraq, Lebanon, and Saudi Arabia.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
